 so once identified computationally those  variants can be studied in wet bench  experiments and we can promote sharing  of the genome sequences with researchers  and with public health groups  and because we were prepared for  large-scale analyses  the team decided to go with a  cloud-hosted pipeline then delivered the  results of that pipeline to a web page  hosted on premises  we also decided to use raw read data in  addition to the assembled sequence data  that's in genbank the raw sequence data  is held in the sequence we read archive  you may have heard that called sra  because it's not averaged to generate a  genome sequence the data allows  identification of major and minor  variants  these records are also extremely large  so it's convenient to take advantage of  technical tools like cloud storage or  machine learning to do analyses and then  we can share the results with a wide  variety of people who may not have  access to those technical tools  besides the amount of data another  challenge that we had was how to  identify the groups of viruses that may  warrant further investigation so people  refer to stars cove two lineages like  pango that you're probably familiar with  those are names like  b.1.617 and they're grouped at fairly  high resolution so sequences within a  pango lineage are pretty similar to each  other  this is also the lineage the  classification scheme that's most used  in research and epidemiological studies  for our purposes though it had a huge  detriment which is that the pangolin  classification tools  can't process unassembled sequences so  we would lose all of that unique data  sets that are in sra so a team at ncbi  developed a method for assigning pango  equivalent lineages  through sets of defining mutations so we  have curators who identify the mutations  that are required for each lineage and  then each mutation must be present in a  particular sequence in order for it to  get classified as that lineage  we've also done some studies so we  verified that the way that we are  assigning the pango classification  has very very high correlation with the  way that sequences like whole genome  sequences are assigned using the um the  classic pangolin tools  the pipeline we ended up with was pretty  complicated it involves many people in  several different teams at ncbi as well  as outside collaborators so in one step  of the pipeline sequence data from  genbank and sra go through a validation  step  contigs are built for unassembled data  and then mutations are identified by  comparing to the refseq in a separate  second step the mutations identified in  sequences are compared to the lineage  defining mutation sets and then sequence  records get classified as tango lineages  finally the data is aggregated with  sample metadata such as the collection  date or location so it can be served up  to accessible cloud directories or our  variants overview website that i'll be  talking about more in a minute  so we have all this data that's coming  out of the pipeline literally millions  of data points a researcher interested  in the data could get lost among the  many files of output and like the saying  goes they might get lost in the trees  and not be able to see the shape of the  forest  so if you've been to the nih campus or  perhaps you've seen a map like  with the the trees and not the roads and  everything highlighted you might  recognize this area though but most  people even if they're familiar with an  area probably won't recognize that top  box just because it's overwhelming even  though it's still accurate  if you label the items that are here you  start to become more familiar with what  you're actually seeing so it becomes  more understandable but you still  maintain accuracy  but if you're only looking for one  particular place it's still too much  information to be useful so you might  want some kind of highlight on only what  you are interested in our goal with the  sars cove two variants overview was like  that we wanted to summarize the  potentially overwhelming sequence data  and help customers find the data that  they're most interested in all without  losing any accuracy  the sars coke 2 overview  sorry the scope 2 variants overview is  an example of how visualizations help  convey the stories in the data  over the past couple of years it's  emphasized that people involved in  pandemic response are often severely  short on time groups may not have access  to a biofermentation  so we could help to support public  health efforts by providing information  on mutation-based lineages and their  spread so this helps to support pandemic  response  public health needs getting information  out to people as quickly as possible and  the information that we're using for  this is really developed through global  efforts to sample and sequence fires  coronavirus ii  so people who are going out there and  doing the swabs and collecting the  samples and really have put tremendous  effort into  making this data publicly available  we identified common questions that a  researcher or someone in public health  might have  we address these questions through our  sars co2 variants overview webpage  this is an interactive dashboard that  summarizes the where when and who  of pango lineage selected by the united  states cdc so this page only includes  variants of interest variants being  monitored variants of concern and  variance of high consequence if we're  ever unfortunate enough to identify one  as mentioned previously this resource is  unique in that it seamlessly combines  unassembled and assembled sequence data  from sra and genbank  as well as the sample descriptors from  biosample and bio project  we also use the biosample data to  identify when a single sample is  represented by data in genbank and sra  so that sample isn't counted twice in  the data that we're showing to customers  and if you're interested in trying this  out you can go to the url that's shown  there or you can search ncbi virus in  most web browsers when that page comes  up you can click that button yellow the  button highlighted by yellow the  variance overview button  okay  so our first user story that we  identified was what mutations are  associated with the lineage and what  risk factors might be associated with  that lineage so all of these lineages  with a cdc status are listed on the left  of the webpage highlighted there in  yellow  customers can search by pango lineage  who greek letter or by cdc status  and each lineage has its own little info  panel showing the lineage-defining  mutation set that was developed by the  curators here at ncbi  the info panels can also be expanded to  show key mutations or in this case  variations because we use the amino acid  positions for the mutations  these are in epitopes or binding sites  identified by our protein domains team  we also link to web pages developed by  another active trace participant called  ncats and they've gathered data on the  effectiveness of therapeutics against  various fires cove 2 lineages  an info panel can be clicked to select a  lineage and then the other displays on  the page will highlight how often the  lineage is sampled and in what locations  several members of active trace are from  pharmaceutical companies and it's  important for them to know which  lineages are growing in the countries  which use their therapeutics so that  they can be sure that their therapeutic  will still continue to work against the  lineage  so we show the frequency of the cdc  status lineages over the past several  months and use a metric called doubling  time or how quickly we expect to have  twice the current number of records to  highlight quickly growing lineages  pop-ups allow customers to dig into the  data and see actual counts as well as  frequency of records which helps with  interpretation  another question many people are  interested in is which lineages have  been detected in